---
layout: layouts/staff.njk
staffPage: mail
staffTitle: Mail
staffHeading: Who hears from <b>you</b>
staffHeadAside: staff-mail-whose.njk
---
{#- MAIL (mockup board 10; was Mailing at /staff/mailing/, which forwards).

   WRITING FIRST. "Write an update" is the first and biggest thing on the page,
   because it is the commonest reason to come here; what has gone out sits
   right under it, every list together; the lists follow, one tab each.

   ONE TAB PER LIST, then the tools that work across all of them.

   The first version put every list in one column and buried the sign-up form
   inside each list's edit panel. That made the page a list OF lists, when what
   somebody actually does here is work ON one — read who is on it, change its
   settings, write to it. A tab per list makes the thing being worked on the
   thing on screen.

   New list sits WITH the lists, because it makes one. A list's settings are
   at the far end of the strip, for the list on screen. The composer is not a
   tab: it opens from Write and fills the page, because writing is one job at
   a time. (The sign-up and contact forms live on Sharing.)

   THE HEADING SAYS "you" ON PURPOSE. Isolation is the feature, and the
   person reading this should see that it applies to them rather than take it
   on faith. -#}

{#- WRITE AN UPDATE: to which lists, the drafts waiting, and Write. -#}
<div class="ml-home" id="mlHome">
  <div class="ml-hero">
    <div class="ml-hero-text">
      <div class="ml-hero-title" data-i18n="ml.write">Write an update</div>
      <div class="ml-hero-to" id="mlHeroTo"></div>
    </div>
    <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="mlDrafts" hidden></button>
    <button type="button" class="solid-btn" id="mlWrite" data-i18n="ml.writeBtn">Write</button>
  </div>

  {#- WHAT HAS GONE OUT, every list together, newest first. A sent mailing is
     a record: its row opens the public copy where the list publishes one. -#}
  <section class="ml-sentall" id="mlSentAll" hidden>
    <span class="sh-lbl" data-i18n="ml.sentAll">Sent</span>
    <div class="ml-sentall-rows" id="mlSentRows"></div>
    <button type="button" class="link-btn" id="mlSentMore" hidden></button>
  </section>

  <div class="ml-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Lists">
    <div class="ml-tabs-lists" id="mlTabs"></div>

    <button type="button" class="ml-tab ml-tab-new" id="mlNewList">
      <span aria-hidden="true">+</span>&nbsp;<span data-i18n="ml.add">New list</span>
    </button>

    <span class="ml-tabs-gap"></span>

    <button type="button" class="ghost-btn ml-settings-btn" id="mlListSettings" aria-pressed="false"
            data-i18n="ml.listSettings" hidden>List settings</button>
  </div>

  <p class="empty" id="mlNoLists" hidden data-i18n="ml.empty">No mailing lists yet.</p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================== A LIST -->
<section class="ml-view" data-view="list" id="mlListView" hidden>
  <!-- ---------- subscribers ---------- -->
  <div class="ml-sub" data-subpanel="people">
    {#- FINDING SOMEBODY AND ADDING SOMEBODY, on one line (board 10): the
       two things done to a list most. A name is added from the person's row
       afterwards, like everything else about them. -#}
    <div class="ml-findadd">
      <input type="search" id="subsQ" class="subs-q"
             data-i18n-attr="placeholder:ml.subsSearch,aria-label:ml.subsSearch" aria-label="Search a name or address"
             placeholder="Search a name or address">
      <form class="ml-add" id="mlAddPerson">
        <input type="email" id="mlNewEmail" maxlength="200" required
               data-i18n-attr="placeholder:ml.addAnEmail,aria-label:ml.addAnEmail"
               aria-label="Add an email address" placeholder="Add an email address">
        <button type="submit" class="ghost-btn" data-i18n="ml.addPerson">Add</button>
      </form>
    </div>
    <span class="hint ml-add-status" id="mlAddStatus"></span>

    {#- The three numbers, then what narrows the table: status, order, tag. -#}
    <div class="subs-bar">
      <div class="ml-counts" id="mlCounts"></div>
      <span class="subs-bar-gap"></span>
      <select id="subsStatus" aria-label="Status">
        <option value="" data-i18n="ml.subsAll">Everyone</option>
        <option value="subscribed" data-i18n="ml.status.subscribed">Subscribed</option>
        <option value="pending" data-i18n="ml.status.pending">Unconfirmed</option>
        <option value="unsubscribed" data-i18n="ml.status.unsubscribed">Unsubscribed</option>
        <option value="bounced" data-i18n="ml.status.bounced">Bounced</option>
      </select>
      <select id="subsSort" aria-label="Sort">
        <option value="" data-i18n="ml.sortNewest">Newest first</option>
        <option value="oldest" data-i18n="ml.sortOldest">Oldest first</option>
        <option value="name" data-i18n="ml.sortName">By name</option>
        <option value="email" data-i18n="ml.sortEmail">By address</option>
        <option value="status" data-i18n="ml.sortStatus">By status</option>
      </select>
      {#- Tags exist to group people, so they have to be a way of finding
         them — a column you can read but not filter by is decoration. -#}
      <select id="subsTag" aria-label="Tag"></select>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn subs-tagbtn" id="subsManageTags"
              data-i18n="ml.manageTags">Tags</button>
      <span class="subs-range" id="subsRange"></span>
    </div>

    {#- THE TAGS BELONG TO THE MINISTRY, not to this list. They are managed
       from here because here is where they are used, but the same set applies
       to every list — which is why this panel is not inside a list's Settings
       tab, where it would read as belonging to one. -#}
    <div class="subs-tags" id="subsTagPanel" hidden>
      <div id="subsTagList"></div>
      <form class="subs-tag-add" id="subsTagAdd">
        <input type="text" id="subsTagName" maxlength="60"
               data-i18n-attr="placeholder:ml.tagName,aria-label:ml.tagName" aria-label="Home church" placeholder="Home church">
        <button type="submit" class="ghost-btn" data-i18n="ml.tagAdd">Add tag</button>
      </form>
    </div>

    {#- A TABLE, not cards. A card per person is readable at ten and unusable
       at three hundred — the eye cannot compare down a column that keeps
       moving. Rows are one line each, so a screen holds about twenty-five
       instead of six. -#}
    {#- ONLY WHEN SOMETHING IS SELECTED. A row of destructive buttons sitting
       there permanently is a row somebody eventually presses without a
       selection in mind. -#}
    <div class="subs-bulk" id="subsBulk" hidden>
      <span class="subs-bulk-n" id="subsBulkCount"></span>
      <span class="subs-bulk-tagwrap" id="subsBulkTagWrap">
        <select id="subsBulkTag" aria-label="Tag"></select>
        <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" data-bulk="tag-add"
                data-i18n="ml.bulkAddTag">Add tag</button>
        <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" data-bulk="tag-remove"
                data-i18n="ml.bulkRemoveTag">Remove tag</button>
      </span>
      <span class="subs-bulk-gap"></span>
      {#- NO "mark subscribed". Marking somebody confirmed is a claim that they
         agreed, and a claim like that made two hundred at a time is how a list
         stops being one people opted into. The single-row picker still allows
         it, one deliberate act at a time. -#}
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" data-bulk="unsubscribed"
              data-i18n="ml.bulkUnsubBtn">Unsubscribe</button>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" data-bulk="bounced"
              data-i18n="ml.bulkBounceBtn">Mark bounced</button>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn del" data-bulk="delete"
              data-i18n="ms.delete">Delete</button>
    </div>

    <div class="subs-wrap">
      <table class="subs" id="mlSubscribers"></table>
    </div>

    <div class="subs-pager" id="subsPager" hidden>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="subsPrev" data-i18n="ml.prev">Previous</button>
      <span class="subs-page" id="subsPageLabel"></span>
      <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="subsNext" data-i18n="ml.next">Next</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ---------- settings ---------- -->
  <div class="ml-sub" data-subpanel="settings" hidden>
    <form id="mlForm">
      <input type="hidden" id="mlId">

      <div class="ms-grid">
        <label class="fld"><span data-i18n="ml.name">Name</span>
          <input type="text" id="mlName" maxlength="120" required>
        </label>
      </div>

      <label class="fld"><span data-i18n="ml.description">Description</span>
        <textarea id="mlDescription" rows="2" maxlength="400"></textarea>
      </label>

      <div class="ms-grid">
        <label class="fld"><span data-i18n="ml.fromName">From name</span>
          <input type="text" id="mlFromName" maxlength="80" required>
        </label>
        {#- CHOSEN, NEVER TYPED. This was a text box, and that is unsafe in a
           way that stays invisible until it matters: the mail provider
           verifies DOMAINS, not addresses, so every address at a verified
           domain sends — including one with a typo in it. It leaves
           successfully, reads as correct in the log, and drops every reply
           into nothing. Nobody finds out until somebody says "I wrote back and
           never heard". A chosen address cannot be mistyped.

           The options are maintained by an administrator on the Partners
           screen, because adding one carries obligations OUTSIDE this system:
           the domain has to be verified with the provider, and anything
           expected to receive has to exist as a mailbox. -#}
        <label class="fld"><span data-i18n="ml.fromEmail">From address</span>
          <select id="mlFromEmail" required></select>
          <span class="fld-hint" id="mlFromNone" hidden></span>
        </label>
        {#- STILL TYPED, deliberately. Replies can go anywhere somebody reads —
           a personal address, a shared inbox, a team alias — and none of those
           need to be a sending address, so restricting this to the list above
           would forbid the ordinary case. -#}
        <label class="fld"><span data-i18n="ml.replyTo">Reply-to</span>
          <input type="email" id="mlReplyTo" maxlength="200"
                 placeholder="you@example.org">
        </label>
      </div>

      <div class="ms-switches">
        <button type="button" class="switch" id="mlOpen" role="switch" aria-checked="false">
          <span class="switch-track"><span class="switch-state">Off</span><span class="switch-knob"></span></span>
          <span class="switch-label"><span data-i18n="ml.open">Open for sign-ups</span>
          </span>
        </button>

        {#- THE ARCHIVE HAS EXISTED AND BEEN UNREACHABLE. mailing_lists
           .archive_public was in the schema, the endpoint accepted it, and
           /archive/… served it — but nothing in the console could switch it
           on, so no partner could ever have used it.

           A LIST-WIDE DECISION, deliberately, not a per-mailing one. A
           newsletter is meant to be read; a prayer update names people and is
           not. Made once and calmly it is safe; made again in a hurry every
           week, one forgetful moment publishes somebody's prayer request. -#}
        <button type="button" class="switch" id="mlArchivePublic" role="switch" aria-checked="false">
          <span class="switch-track"><span class="switch-state">Off</span><span class="switch-knob"></span></span>
          <span class="switch-label"><span data-i18n="ml.archivePublic">Past issues readable on the web</span>
          </span>
        </button>
      </div>

      <div class="ms-actions">
        <button type="submit" class="solid-btn" data-i18n="ml.save">Save list</button>
        <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="mlCancel" data-i18n="ms.cancel">Cancel</button>
        <button type="button" class="del" id="mlArchive" data-i18n="ml.archive">Archive</button>
        <span class="hint" id="mlFormStatus"></span>
      </div>
    </form>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ COMPOSER -->
{#- WRITING ON THE LEFT, WHAT ARRIVES ON THE RIGHT.

   The preview is a frame running the real renderer, not an impression of one.
   Email is the medium where what you wrote and what people see diverge most —
   Gmail deletes stylesheets, Outlook renders through Word — so a preview built
   from the editor's own DOM would show a version of the message that exists
   nowhere.

   The toolbar is deliberately short. Every button here is a tag that survives
   in every mail client; the ones missing are missing because they do not.
   Text color, font family and font size are absent for that reason — they
   render three different ways and the third is unreadable. -#}
<section class="ml-view" data-view="composer" id="mlComposerView" hidden>
  <button type="button" class="ml-back" id="mlBack">&larr;&nbsp;<span data-i18n="nav.mail">Mail</span></button>

  {#- NO "To" FIELD, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN.

     A composer with To / Cc / Bcc is a personal mail client. This one sends to
     a LIST: every confirmed subscriber gets their own separate message with
     their own unsubscribe link and List-Unsubscribe header. One message to
     many could only carry one unsubscribe link — removing whoever pressed it
     from somebody else's row, or nobody's — a typed address would bypass the
     double opt-in the whole system rests on, and bulk Bcc is a well-known spam
     signal that would damage the sending domain for every list on it.

     So the recipient row is a picker, and the count beside it is real. -#}
  <div class="cp-bar">
    <label class="cp-pick"><span data-i18n="ml.cpWriting">Sending to</span>
      <select id="cpList"></select>
    </label>
    <span class="cp-count" id="cpCount"></span>

    <label class="cp-pick cp-pick-draft"><span data-i18n="ml.cpDraftLabel">Draft</span>
      <select id="cpDraft" aria-label="Draft"></select>
    </label>
    <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="cpNew" data-i18n="ml.cpNew">New</button>
    {#- THE ONE THING WORTH KEEPING from the preview panel. Gmail cuts a
       message off at about 102KB and shows "Message clipped" — and because the
       cut can land mid-tag, everything after it can fail to render. Learning
       that at the moment of sending would be learning it too late. -#}
    <span class="cp-size" id="cpSize"></span>
    <span class="cp-state" id="cpState"></span>
  </div>

  <p class="empty" id="cpNoLists" hidden data-i18n="ml.empty">No mailing lists yet.</p>

  {#- ONE COLUMN. There was a live preview beside this, showing the real
     rendered email in a frame. It is gone, and its own warning label is the
     reason: a browser is not a mail client, so the preview could only ever be
     a layout check — while the test send shows the actual thing in the actual
     inbox. Two answers to one question, one of which could mislead, and the
     honest one already existed.

     What it took with it was the excuse for a white card. The writing surface
     now uses the console's own colors, because it is part of the console. -#}
  <div class="cp-split" id="cpSplit" hidden>
    <div class="cp-write">
      <div class="cp-card">
        <div class="cp-fields">
          <label class="cp-field">
            <span data-i18n="ml.cpSubject">Subject</span>
            <input type="text" id="cpSubject" maxlength="200"
                   data-i18n-attr="placeholder:ml.cpSubjectHint"
                   placeholder="What this update is about">
          </label>
          {#- The line an inbox shows beside the subject. Left empty, mail
             clients scrape the first words of the body — which is why so much
             mail previews as "View this in your browser". -#}
          <label class="cp-field">
            <span data-i18n="ml.cpPreheader">Preview line</span>
            <input type="text" id="cpPreheader" maxlength="160"
                   data-i18n-attr="placeholder:ml.cpPreheaderHint2"
                   placeholder="The line shown next to the subject in an inbox">
          </label>
        </div>

        {#- EVERY BUTTON HERE IS REAL and shows whether it is currently on.
           `aria-pressed` is maintained from the editor's own state on both
           content changes AND cursor movement — clicking into bold text with
           no typing lights the Bold button. That is the acceptance test for
           this toolbar, and the bug that sank the two editors before it.

           Constrained to what email actually renders. No font family, no
           arbitrary color, no columns: offering a control the output cannot
           honor is worse than not offering it. -#}
        <div class="cp-tools" role="toolbar" aria-label="Formatting">
          <button type="button" data-tool="bold" aria-pressed="false" title="Bold"><b>B</b></button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="italic" aria-pressed="false" title="Italic"><i>I</i></button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="underline" aria-pressed="false" title="Underline"><u>U</u></button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="strike" aria-pressed="false" title="Strikethrough"><s>S</s></button>

          <span class="cp-tools-sep"></span>
          <button type="button" data-tool="larger" aria-pressed="false" title="Larger">A&#8593;</button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="smaller" aria-pressed="false" title="Smaller">A&#8595;</button>
          {#- The ministry's own accent, stored as intent rather than as a
             color value — so it stays right after a rebrand, and a draft
             copied between ministries is not wearing the wrong one. -#}
          {#- The only button in this toolbar with nothing inside it: the others
             are named by the letter they show (B, I, U, S). A title alone is
             an unreliable accessible name, so this one says it outright. -#}
          <button type="button" data-tool="accent" aria-pressed="false"
                  title="Brand color" aria-label="Brand color"
                  class="cp-swatch"><span></span></button>

          <span class="cp-tools-sep"></span>
          <button type="button" data-tool="h2" aria-pressed="false" title="Heading">H1</button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="h3" aria-pressed="false" title="Sub-heading">H2</button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="bullet" aria-pressed="false" title="Bullets">&bull;</button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="ordered" aria-pressed="false" title="Numbers">1.</button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="quote" aria-pressed="false" title="Quote">&rdquo;</button>

          <span class="cp-tools-sep"></span>
          <button type="button" data-cmd="link" aria-pressed="false" title="Link">&#128279;</button>
          <button type="button" data-cmd="image" title="Picture in the message">&#128247;</button>
          <button type="button" data-tool="rule" title="Divider">&mdash;</button>
        </div>

        <div id="cpBody" class="cp-body"></div>
      </div>

      {#- A DIFFERENT MECHANISM FROM AN INLINE PICTURE, and conflating the two
         is the usual mistake. A picture is fetched by the reader's mail client
         from a URL in the body. An attachment travels WITH the message, never
         appears in the HTML, and is handed to the mail provider separately —
         so it costs its full weight in every copy sent. Shown only when there
         is something to show. -#}
      <div class="cp-files" id="cpFiles" hidden>
        <span class="adm-label" data-i18n="ml.cpAttached">Attached files</span>
        <ul id="cpFileList"></ul>
      </div>

      <div class="cp-actions">
        <button type="button" class="solid-btn cp-send" id="cpSend" data-i18n="ml.cpSend">Send</button>
        <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="cpSave" data-i18n="ml.cpSave">Save draft</button>
        <button type="button" class="ghost-btn" id="cpTest" data-i18n="ml.cpTest">Send me a test</button>
        <span class="cp-actions-gap"></span>
        <button type="button" class="cp-icon" id="cpAttach"
                data-i18n-attr="title:ml.cpAttach" title="Attach a file">&#128206;</button>
        <button type="button" class="cp-icon cp-icon-del" id="cpDelete"
                data-i18n-attr="title:ml.cpDelete" title="Delete draft">&#128465;</button>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>

</section>
